from pathlib import Path
p=Path('build_manuscript.py');s=p.read_text()
s=s.replace('FA MAS Contract Aware Recovery for Stateful LLM Agent Service Compositions','FA MAS Contract Aware Recovery for Stateful Agent Service Workflows')
s=s.replace('These engineered results do not constitute live-LLM or production evidence.','A post-audit matched sensitivity study holds diagnosis probabilities equal and applies a stricter orphan-state judge; it does not reproduce a safety advantage over simpler recovery controls. The results are model-free and do not establish live-LLM or production superiority.')
s=s.replace('but these strands do not jointly specify service contracts, end-to-end service-level objectives, dynamic recomposition, and compensation-safe recovery.','and prior transactional-agent systems already combine validation, persistent state and compensation. The remaining question is how explicit service contracts constrain recovery choices under resource limits, and what evidence can support those choices.')
s=s.replace('We present FA-MAS, a failure-aware runtime that treats agent execution as a stateful service composition.','We present FA-MAS, a reference contract-enforcement controller and a broader recovery design for stateful service workflows.')
s=s.replace('It combines machine-checkable functional, operational, state, recovery, security, and quality contracts with trace-grounded diagnosis and constrained recovery selection.','The design specifies functional, operational, state, recovery, security and quality clauses; the executable studies implement a reduced subset of deterministic gates and authored recovery estimates.')
s=s.replace('Recovery actions include retry, service substitution, subgraph recomposition, checkpoint resume, compensation, abstention, and escalation.','The executable controls support retry, status reconciliation, substitution, compensation and stopping. Subgraph recomposition, calibrated diagnosis and multi-agent coordination remain design extensions.')
s=s.replace('A durable state layer records intent, idempotency keys, outcomes, checkpoints, and saga-style compensations.','The local service implementations persist effect state and stable intent keys in SQLite; they do not establish the full write-ahead/checkpoint architecture.')
s=s.replace('FA-MAS increases acceptable outcomes by 1.8–12.8 percentage points relative to tested baselines and reduces unsafe outcomes by 4.2–12.4 points, while raw completion remains 1.0–1.6 points below stronger adaptive baselines.','Under unequal authored diagnosis parameters and the original terminal metric, FA-MAS increases acceptable outcomes by 1.8–12.8 percentage points and reduces recorded unsafe outcomes by 4.2–12.4 points; these contrasts do not isolate contract enforcement.')
s=s.replace('The paper makes four scoped contributions. First, it defines a service contract that joins input and output schemas with preconditions, postconditions, invariants, idempotency class, compensation, authorization scope, latency, cost, and quality evidence. Second, it formulates recovery as constrained selection over a recomposition frontier rather than unrestricted replanning. Third, it specifies compensation-safe execution using write-ahead intent, idempotency keys, outcome reconciliation, checkpoints, and resumable sagas. Fourth, it provides a reproducible fault-injection artifact with paired seeds, explicit baselines, ablations, raw traces, and multiplicity-adjusted comparisons.','The paper contributes a formal contract-constrained recovery specification, a finite-action Pareto selector, two executable local service studies with terminal state judges, and a reproducible evaluation artifact. The specification includes a broader subgraph frontier, write-ahead/checkpoint protocol and multi-agent roles; these are not claimed as fully implemented. The measured contribution is mechanism behavior and disclosed failure cases, not universal performance improvement.')
s=s.replace('The unresolved joint problem is to recover a dynamically composed agent workflow when evidence is uncertain, side effects may be ambiguous, and every action consumes shared latency, monetary, permission, and risk budgets.','Transactional-agent recovery is already addressed by SagaLLM [21] and Atomix [22]. We examine a narrower contract-enforcement and recovery-selection question; novelty cannot rest on introducing compensation, validation or durable agent state.')
s=s.replace('The technical center is the recomposition frontier: a set of recovery plans that are contract-valid, policy-authorized, state-safe, and nondominated under predicted success, latency, cost, and risk.','The proposed technical center is a contract-valid, policy-authorized recovery frontier over success, latency, cost and risk. The executable selector currently prunes a finite set of single-action estimates; it does not enumerate executable recovery subgraphs.')
s=s.replace('Twenty current unit tests','Thirty-eight current unit tests')
s=s.replace('The paper\'s defensible contribution is therefore not a universal claim of superior agent intelligence.','The paper\'s defensible contribution is a reference specification and transparent mechanism evaluation; the matched negative result prevents a claim of demonstrated safety superiority.')
s=s.replace('In the controlled simulator, FA-MAS improves acceptable outcomes and substantially reduces unsafe outcomes relative to six baselines, but it does not maximize raw completion against the strongest unconstrained recovery policies.','The original simulator favors FA-MAS on recorded safety under unequal authored diagnosis parameters. With matched diagnosis and a stricter orphan-state criterion, FA-MAS does not improve safety over the tested simpler controls. No live-model advantage has been measured.')
s=s.replace('Its central mechanism combines machine-checkable contracts, trace-grounded failure belief, a nondominated recomposition frontier, and compensation-safe durable state.','The design combines contracts, failure-belief estimation, a proposed recomposition frontier and compensation-aware state recovery. Only reduced gates, finite-action selection and local service state are currently executable.')
# Add primary transactional references and focused comparisons without claiming empirical superiority.
s=s.replace('"Online access dates and final bibliographic metadata should be checked before submission."', '"[21] E. Y. Chang and L. Geng, “SagaLLM: Context Management, Validation, and Transaction Guarantees for Multi-Agent LLM Planning,” arXiv:2503.11951v3, 2025.",\n"[22] B. Mohammadi et al., “Atomix: Timely, Transactional Tool Use for Reliable Agentic Workflows,” arXiv:2602.14849v2, 2026.",\n"[23] K. Wang et al., “BRA-Audit: Budgeted Runtime Auditing for LLM Multi-Agent Systems via Cumulative-Exposure Audit-Point Placement,” arXiv:2608.14668v1, 2026.",\n"[24] V. Srinivasan, “A Methodology for Selecting and Composing Runtime Architecture Patterns for Production LLM Agents,” arXiv:2605.20173v2, 2026."')
needle="for page in PAGES:\n    page['paras'].extend(EXTRA.get(page['title'], []))"
addition='''for page in PAGES:
    if page['title']=='II RELATED WORK AND GAP':
        page['paras'].extend([
            "SagaLLM [21] integrates saga-style recovery, persistent context and independent validation agents. These capabilities overlap directly with the proposed state/compensation/validation architecture. FA-MAS cannot claim novelty for that integration alone; its proposed distinction is explicit feasibility and multi-objective recovery selection, whose full subgraph implementation remains future work.",
            "Atomix [22] coordinates tool-effect settlement using progress-aware transactions and per-resource frontiers, with abort and compensation for effects that can be reversed. Its frontier tracks whether earlier conflicting work can arrive; the proposed FA-MAS frontier ranks feasible recovery choices by estimated success and resource/risk demand. These are different objects, not evidence of superiority. Our local mechanism studies do not reproduce Atomix's concurrency or irreversible-effect evaluation.",
            "BRA-Audit [23] studies budgeted dependency-aware auditing and localized recovery. Runtime-pattern work [24] formalizes proposer/verifier/commit boundaries and saga/gate patterns. Therefore budgeted oversight and deterministic commit gates are also established concepts. The current contribution remains an explicit contract-controlled reference and auditable experimental boundary; a comparative systems advantage remains unproven."
        ])
    if page['title']=='IX RESULTS CONTINUED':
        page['paras'].append("Post-audit matched sensitivity. An additional exploratory study executes 18,000 rows with equal detection/localization probabilities (0.82/0.76), no contract detection bonus, and the same recovery operations except for the stated ablation. A stricter judge counts active writes left by unfinished workflows as unsafe. Full FA-MAS completes 85.00%, accepts 87.09% and records 9.96% unsafe outcomes. Removing gates completes 86.82%, accepts 86.82% and records 9.73% unsafe. First-feasible selection completes 85.49%, accepts 87.58% and records 8.93% unsafe; removing compensation completes 87.71%, accepts 89.80% and records 7.60% unsafe. These negative findings reject a demonstrated safety advantage in this matched simulator. The study is post-audit exploratory, not preregistered; the original 52,500 rows remain unchanged historical results under their weaker terminal criterion.")
    if page['title']=='VII IMPLEMENTATION':
        page['paras'].append("The reference Pareto selector now explicitly removes dominated finite-action candidates before monotone utility ranking. Tests establish dominance behavior and preservation of maximal utility for nonnegative weights. This validates a selection component, not calibrated estimates, executable-subgraph enumeration, or the full proposed architecture. The matched controls use identical diagnosis inputs and disclose a stricter terminal-state interpretation.")
    if page['title']=='X LIVE MODEL CONFIRMATORY PROTOCOL':
        page['paras']=["The earlier unexecuted single-decision Tier-2 plan is retained as a historical artifact. The new bounded end-to-end protocol uses real loopback HTTP/SQLite services and lets a tool-calling model choose every operation. It specifies 32 held-out task instances, two model classes and two policies, yielding 128 trajectories with at most 12 turns each (1,536 calls). Both controllers receive the same prompt, contracts, tools, stable intent keys and budgets; FA-MAS adds prerequisite, substitute-equivalence, replacement and capture gates. No live model calls have been made.",
                       "The held-out instances change workflow length, SKU and capacity; they are authored variants of known fault families, not unseen production tasks. Four development tasks are disjoint. Thirty-eight unit tests pass and eight scripted development trajectories complete, all marked non-live. The lock covers code, task bank, model endpoints and rates, and runtime versions. Provider failures remain in the data; incomplete matrices cannot enter the live comparison. Local hashing is not independent preregistration.",
                       "Analysis pairs task/model cells and clusters uncertainty by the 16 service/fault families. These small authored clusters do not provide demonstrated power for small effects or population estimates. The study tests one tool-using model at a time and does not validate multi-agent coordination or the full recovery frontier. Model IDs, endpoints, credentials and an approved billing ceiling remain execution gates."]
'''
assert needle in s;s=s.replace(needle,addition+'\n'+needle)
